            "body": "## Summary\n\nThe diff adds `_token_count_guard` and calls it from `_eligible_for_removal` with the **literal** `0`. That makes the guard always fail, so every removal path short-circuits before root-netuid, probe, or grace checks.\n\n## Findings\n\n### F1 — Removals permanently disabled (`never-expires`) — **blocking**\n\n**Measured:** At head, `_eligible_for_removal` begins with:\n\n```python\nif not _token_count_guard(0):\n    return False\n```\n\nand `_token_count_guard` returns `token_count > 0`.\n\n**Inferred:** For any `netuid`, evidence, and `now`, `_token_count_guard(0)` is `False`, so the function always returns `False` on the first line. `_compute_membership` only moves a negative netuid into `dropped` when `_eligible_for_removal` is true; `_shrink_allowed` requires the same for each netuid leaving the prior snapshot. After the 48h grace (`STALE_GRACE_SECONDS`), chain-negative members that should leave instead stay eligible only in the negative-in-map branch and never complete removal — membership shrink via evidence is blocked every cycle.\n\n**Measured:** `DropEvidence` still has only `tmc`, `probe`, and `negative_since`; there is no `token_count` on evidence and no other head file references `token_count` in this fixture tree. The PR title’s audit-trail gating is not wired to any consumed value.\n\n### F2 — Likely implementation mistake (not blocking alone)\n\n**Guess:** The author may have intended `_token_count_guard(<audit token count>)` from a row or audit record, but passing constant `0` is indistinguishable from an intentional kill-switch on all removals.\n\n## Owner rules touchpoints\n\n**Inferred:** Standing behavior in this module requires removals only after probe-false evidence and grace (`_eligible_for_removal`). Disabling that gate holds stale netuids in the shared universe and forces `_shrink_allowed` false whenever a shrink would occur — conflicting with chain-wins negative handling that expects eventual shrink after grace.\n\n## Settled-check\n\n**Measured:** No roadmap/docs in the available head tree for this PR; nothing settles “always pass 0 to token guard.” Treat F1 as new.\n\n## Verdict rationale\n\nThis is **FAIL**, not PASS: F1 is `never-expires` via the real entry point `_eligible_for_removal` → `_compute_membership` / `_shrink_allowed` → `SubnetUniverseProvider.refresh_once`.",
